{"page_content": "While the paper was written for the Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames (E-FIELD \nFlames) experiment, much of the discussion applies to ACME in general, including the Flame \nDesign investigation.  The figure referenced in this excerpt is not included herein.  Clarifications \nand additional information, not in the conference paper, are included in boxed notes like this. \n \nThe CIR experiments are configured, but not conducted, by the ISS crew.  Thus far, ACME\u2019s activity \nhas included the majority of the crew members, regardless of their nationalities.  Most often the \nastronauts and cosmonauts exchange gas bottles, mass flow controllers, and igniter tips.  In the \nlatter case, the igniter tip exchange can occur after failure or proactively before failure when the \ncrew already has changes to make changes in the interior of the insert.  While these activities may \nseem mundane, the crew play a critical role in setting up the hardware for each experiment.  As \nan extreme example, there were crew activities on 8 separate days in Sept.-Oct. 2017 to configure \nthe CIR and ACME hardware for the first ACME experiment, namely the Coflow Laminar Diffusion \nFlame (CLD Flame) experiment.  In contrast, only two crew days were required to reconfigure the", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 20}}